# Markdown that grows with you

::::box{class=lede}
Every HyMarkX document is a Markdown document. It stays one until the day you need a component,
a value, or a button — and then it is still the same file.
::::

::::box{class=cta}
`npm install hymarkx` [read the docs](https://github.com/NAME0x0/HyMarkX/tree/main/docs/guides) [browse the source](https://github.com/NAME0x0/HyMarkX)
::::

::::

::::box{as=section class="chapter reveal" #how}

::::box{class=eyebrow}
The same file, four times
::::

## Nothing to migrate to

::::box{class=lead}
There is no point where you stop writing Markdown and start writing a program. Each step below
is the one before it with a line added. Move through them.
::::

::::box{class=stepper}

::::box{class=stepper-bar}

:::box{class=rail}

:::button{class={step == 1 ? "rail-step on" : (step > 1 ? "rail-step done" : "rail-step")} on-click="step = 1"}
Markdown
:::

:::button{class={step == 2 ? "rail-step on" : (step > 2 ? "rail-step done" : "rail-step")} on-click="step = 2"}
A component
:::

:::button{class={step == 3 ? "rail-step on" : (step > 3 ? "rail-step done" : "rail-step")} on-click="step = 3"}
Values
:::

:::button{class={step == 4 ? "rail-step on" : "rail-step"} on-click="step = 4"}
State
:::

:::

:::box{class=stepper-controls}

:::button{on-click="step = step - 1 < 1 ? 4 : step - 1"}
Back
:::

:::button{on-click="step = step + 1 > 4 ? 1 : step + 1"}
Next
:::

:::

::::

::::box{class={step == 1 ? "step live" : "step"}}

:::box{class=step-note}
Plain CommonMark. Nothing on this step is HyMarkX, and the output is byte for byte what any
other Markdown processor would give you.
:::

:::box{class=pane}
### You write

```md
### Release notes

Shipping on **Friday**.
```
:::

:::box{class=preview}
### You get

### Release notes

Shipping on **Friday**.
:::

::::

::::box{class={step == 2 ? "step live" : "step"}}

:::box{class=step-note}
Three lines added, and the file is still a Markdown file. Only the components this document
used bring any CSS with them.
:::

:::box{class=pane}
### You write

```md
### Release notes

:::note[Heads up]{type=warning}
Deployment starts at 8 PM UTC.
:::
```
:::

:::box{class=preview}
### You get

### Release notes

:::note[Heads up]{type=warning}
Deployment starts at 8 PM UTC.
:::
:::

::::

::::box{class={step == 3 ? "step live" : "step"}}

:::box{class=step-note}
Frontmatter, read back in the prose. Every value resolves while compiling, so this step still
emits no JavaScript at all.
:::

:::box{class=pane}
### You write

```md
---
quarter: Q3
revenue: 42500
---

### Results for {{ quarter }}

Revenue was {{ revenue }}.
```
:::

:::box{class=preview}
### You get

### Results for Q3

Revenue was 42500.
:::

::::

::::box{class={step == 4 ? "step live" : "step"}}

:::box{class=step-note}
The first step that costs anything: state and an event handler bring in the 591-byte runtime.
The button below is the real one — this page is that file.
:::

:::box{class=pane}
### You write

```md
::state{count=0}

:::button{on-click="count = count + 1"}
Increment
:::

Clicked {{ count }} times.
```
:::

:::box{class=preview}
### You get

:::button{on-click="count = count + 1"}
Increment
:::

Clicked {{ count }} times.
:::

::::

